{"assets":["/editorial-assets/3b4a1581345073a9af45ecbc3748ccefeb55b25c05b1fd481c562d9ee6cf15a2.png","/editorial-assets/90b774752a46087ed1b73deb1553ea7d8f86a72c0568bed385778ce4a9f91bea.png","/editorial-assets/f676a6b25ca211660fa2223115fb4d9073ab35477c638321d79b482ac5ba9039.png","/editorial-assets/c45f50ae8f81ae1231706c25a24bb2d1e8e0d70fb4cd53afd9d4650a3acb8f9c.png","/editorial-assets/99177fcc886275a2988cde17b54c4e263841995b64665e2870d65767170a6120.png","/editorial-assets/e9a708f608bb4bbd62f9570ec6781c44dceb45fb8c371600b26e84b53314da42.png","/editorial-assets/172a7360b5767852029c0a34e2eb342724318582de1ce9d60b09e67690831509.png","/editorial-assets/e981ba2ad3c2d9675257688342c0dfd1cf003b54af06e56ec798be8fad7fdb57.png","/editorial-assets/1ffc5acf47942102527cdf09f3fa1333ab16adacd7a70025ed1f3c7abcafac8b.png","/editorial-assets/9d47c550cf9df59524d185c344f81e670cf2d09fe57c987a7239214f54da7222.png","/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png","/editorial-assets/837343a6f5e4e7d44115f7512f249454bfa64572633db30c03c24aedd757ce23.png"],"contestId":"725","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here are the authors of the problems:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A — Written by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/GlebsHP"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"GlebsHP"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", prepared by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/vovuh"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"vovuh"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B — Written by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/GlebsHP"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"GlebsHP"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", prepared by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/vovuh"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"vovuh"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/zxqfl"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zxqfl"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Errichto"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Errichto"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/zxqfl"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zxqfl"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/zxqfl"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zxqfl"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"G — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/KAN"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"KAN"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I'd like to thank the Codeforces team for their help, particularly "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/KAN"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"KAN"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", who was the tester for this round."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725A - Jumping Ball"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's divide the sequence into three parts: a sequence (possibly, empty) of '<' bumpers in the beginning, a sequence (possibly, empty) of '>' bumpers in the end, and a sequence (possibly, empty) of any type bumpers between the first and the second parts. It can be easily seen that if the ball starts in the left part, it falls from the left side; if the ball starts in the right part, it falls from the right side; if the ball starts anywhere in the middle part, it never reaches the other parts and doesn't fall from the field."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Thus, the answer is the number of '<' bumpers in the beginning plus the number of '>' bumpers in the end. The complexity of the solution is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725B - Food on the Plane"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The only observation one has to make to solve this problem is that flight attendants move 4 rows forward at 16 seconds. Here, 6 seconds are required to serve everyone in one row, then 1 second to move forward, 6 seconds to serve one more row, then 3 more seconds to move. Thus, 6 + 1 + 6 + 3 = 16 in total."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now, if we are interested in seat "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in row "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", the answer is equal to the answer for seat "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in row "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"xmod"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"4 + 1 plus "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3b4a1581345073a9af45ecbc3748ccefeb55b25c05b1fd481c562d9ee6cf15a2.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Note, that we use integer division, so it's not equal to ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)·4."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Solution: compute the serving time for first 4 rows. Find the answer using formula above."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725C - Hidden Word"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. The input string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" contains 27 letters and it contains every letter in the English alphabet. The English alphabet has 26 letters, so there is exactly one repeated letter."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. If the instances of the repeated letter are adjacent in the string, there can be no solution. That's because every English letter occurs in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and the solution grid contains exactly 26 tiles, so any solution must have all letters distinct. Consider the tile containing the repeated letter. None of the tiles adjacent to it can contain the repeated letter because the letters of the grid are distinct, so the grid doesn't contain "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3. Otherwise, there is a solution. Delete the second occurrence of the repeated letter and write the resulting string in circular order. For example, if the string with the repeated letter deleted is ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ, we write this to the grid:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"ABCDEFGHIJKLMZYXWVUTSRQPON"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then we rotate the grid, one step at a time, until it contains "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". For example, if the above grid isn't a valid solution, we next try this:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"ZABCDEFGHIJKLYXWVUTSRQPONM"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"And then this:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"YZABCDEFGHIJKXWVUTSRQPONML"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This will eventually give us a solution. Suppose the repeated letter lies between G and H in the original string. In each rotation, there are 2 letters adjacent to both G and H in the grid (in the above examples these letters are S and T, then Q and R, then O and P). When we rotate the string by a step, the adjacent letters advance by 1 or 2 steps. Eventually the repeated letter will be adjacent to both G and H, and this will yield a solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725D - Contest Balloons"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I (a problem author) want to thank the ICPC Live staff. During the World Finals a commentator ([user:Egor] maybe?) said that contests always have balloons and maybe someone should invent a problem about them for the next finals (I'm not organizing those though). Thanks for the idea!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If you just want the solution, read only the last paragraph. Previous ones describe how to find a correct approach. It may lead to a slightly more complicated solution, but you will learn something for sure."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To come up with a solution, you must try iterating over some value or maybe binary searching an optimal value. Let's list a few reasonable ideas. Try to binary search the answer (for fixed final place of Limak you must say if he can get at least this place). Or iterate over the number of disqualified teams (if Limak should beat exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" teams, what is the best place he can get?). Or iterate over the number of balloons Limak should give away."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The last idea turns out to be fruitful. The first observation is that you don't have to consider every possible number of balloons to give away. We can assume that the final number of balloons will be either 0 or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" for some "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Otherwise Limak could give away some more balloons and his place won't be worse (if there are no more teams, let's imagine he can destroy them). We use here a simple observation that the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th team either will be disqualified or will still have exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" balloons, i.e. values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" won't change."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") possibilities for the final number of balloons. Let's first solve a problem in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/90b774752a46087ed1b73deb1553ea7d8f86a72c0568bed385778ce4a9f91bea.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" by considering possibilities separately, each in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f676a6b25ca211660fa2223115fb4d9073ab35477c638321d79b482ac5ba9039.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's say Limak will have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" balloons at the end. He can give away "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" balloons. He should try to get rid of teams with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" because worse teams don't affect his place. You should sort teams with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by the number of balloons they need to get disqualified i.e. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. It's easiest for Limak to get rid of teams with small value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 so you should just check how many of those values can be chosen so that the sum of them won't exceed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". It turns out to be possible to improve the above by considering possibilities in the decreasing order. Think what happens when you decrease "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Some more teams become a possible target and you should consider their values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. A sorted set of those values should be useful. Try to invent a solution yourself now. Then you can see one simple version in the next paragraph."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now let's see an "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f676a6b25ca211660fa2223115fb4d9073ab35477c638321d79b482ac5ba9039.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" greedy solution. Create a variable "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" representing the final number of balloons Limak has, initially "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Create and maintain a multiset with values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 of teams better than the current "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", i.e. teams with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". If it makes sense to give away some more balloons, Limak has to eliminate at least one of those better teams (otherwise he can stop now). It's easiest to eliminate a team with the smallest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, so we can just remove the smallest element from the set and decrease "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by its value. Maybe some more teams become better than the current "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and you should add those to the multiset. Note that the multiset contains exactly those teams which would win with Limak in the current scenario, so after every change of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" you can update the answer 'ans = min(ans, my_multiset.size() + 1)'. Remember to stop if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" becomes smaller than 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725E - Too Much Money"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. If there is a solution where Bob gives Alfred "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" coins, then there is a solution where Bob gives Alfred only one coin. We can prove it by induction on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The base case "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1 is trivial. In the inductive case, let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the highest-valued coins that Bob uses (where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). The sequence of coins chosen by Alfred looks like this: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I claim that if we replace "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with a single coin worth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" dollars then we will still have a solution. Clearly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will be chosen by Alfred no later than Alfred chose "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the original sequence. So if some coin would have caused Alfred to have more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" dollars and so was skipped by Alfred, it will still be skipped by Alfred in the new solution since Alfred has no less money in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" than he did in the original solution. Likewise, we know that "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c45f50ae8f81ae1231706c25a24bb2d1e8e0d70fb4cd53afd9d4650a3acb8f9c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" so if coin was chosen in the original solution then it will still be chosen in the new solution."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. After pre-processing the input, we can simulate Alfred's algorithm in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/99177fcc886275a2988cde17b54c4e263841995b64665e2870d65767170a6120.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" with the following process: find the next coin that Alfred will use (this can be done in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1) with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") pre-processing). Suppose there are x copies of this coin in the input, the coin is worth y dollars, and Alfred has z dollars. Then Alfred will use "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") copies of this coin. We can find this value in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1). Now suppose this algorithm has run for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" steps. Then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" has taken "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" distinct values and Alfred has used at least one coin of each value, so the coins in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are worth at least "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) / 2. Thus, after "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/99177fcc886275a2988cde17b54c4e263841995b64665e2870d65767170a6120.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" steps the value of the coins in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will exceed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So this process runs in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/99177fcc886275a2988cde17b54c4e263841995b64665e2870d65767170a6120.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" time."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3. Try every possible solution from 1 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and check if it works. The complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e9a708f608bb4bbd62f9570ec6781c44dceb45fb8c371600b26e84b53314da42.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725F - Family Photos"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. We are going to transform the stacks in such a way that every move is no worse than passing."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. Consider a stack with values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". (The top photo is worth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for Alice and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for Bonnie and the bottom photo is worth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for Alice and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for Bonnie.) If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" then taking the top photo is no worse than passing. We can prove it by rearranging the inequality: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the value of the stack, from Alice's perspective, if Alice gets to move first. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the value, from Alice's perspective, if Bonnie gets to move first. So Alice doesn't incur a loss by making the first move, which means that a move here is no worse than passing. By symmetry, the same argument applies to Bonnie."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" then both players would rather have the other player make the first move. However, this doesn't necessarily mean that the stack will be ignored by the players. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" then Alice can still profit by making the first move here. Since it would be even better for Alice if Bonnie made the first move, Alice can ignore this stack until Bonnie passes, and only then make a move here. So if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" then this stack is equivalent to a single photo with value ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"4. Likewise, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" then the stack is equivalent to a single photo with value ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and neither (3) nor (4) apply, both players will ignore the stack and we can simply delete it."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6. Now every move is no worse than passing, so every photo will eventually be taken. For every photo ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), replace it with a photo "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/172a7360b5767852029c0a34e2eb342724318582de1ce9d60b09e67690831509.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and add "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e981ba2ad3c2d9675257688342c0dfd1cf003b54af06e56ec798be8fad7fdb57.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the final answer. This is equivalent to the original problem because "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/1ffc5acf47942102527cdf09f3fa1333ab16adacd7a70025ed1f3c7abcafac8b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/9d47c550cf9df59524d185c344f81e670cf2d09fe57c987a7239214f54da7222.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7. Now every photo is worth the same to both players, so we can sort them in descending order and assign the photos with even indices to Alice and the photos with odd indices to Bonnie. Since every stack in the transformed problem has "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", this guarantees that the top photo will be taken before the bottom photo in each stack. The complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"8. Actually, this approach can be extended to solve the problem when each stack has any number of photos. The code linked below solves this more general problem. This problem wasn't asked in the contest because it was too similar to "},{"attrs":{"href":"http://main.edu.pl/en/archive/pa/2010/ter"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"http://main.edu.pl/en/archive/pa/2010/ter"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":". You can find the solution for this problem at "},{"attrs":{"href":"http://www.mimuw.edu.pl/ idziaszek/termity/termity.pdf"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"http://www.mimuw.edu.pl/ idziaszek/termity/termity.pdf"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Code: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/submission/21731928"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"http://codeforces.com/contest/725/submission/21731928"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"725G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/725/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"725G - Messages on a Tree"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hint: Sort the queries somehow so that the answer on every query will depend only on previous queries. After that, use heavy-light decomposition to answer the queries quickly."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Solution: First, sort the queries by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"] + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] is the depth of the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". It can be easily shown that the answer for every query depends only on the previous ones, so we can proceed them one by one."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also, after the sort we can assume that any query blocks the path from the initiator to the node that rejects the query in one instant, so we can only care about unblocking times. Suppose some node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" unblocks at the moment "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Which queries will be rejected by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" if they reach it? Obviously, the queries which have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" on the path from the initiator to the root and for which "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"] - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" holds. Let's rewrite this as follows: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. Now the left part depends only on the blocking node, and the right part depends only on the query. Let's define "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we have an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") solution: we proceed queries one by one, for each query we find such a node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" on the path from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the root so that the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"] holds for the node, and it has the maximum depth, and then reassign values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" on the path from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To make the solution faster, we have to construct the heavy-light decomposition on the tree. Now let's store the values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" in segment trees on the decomposed paths. To assign on the path we should note that the values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" form arithmetical progression. To query the deepest node we should store the maximum value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" in every node of the segment trees. This solution works in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/837343a6f5e4e7d44115f7512f249454bfa64572633db30c03c24aedd757ce23.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/47974"}